## Product Mental Model

Figma Make should understand Spotler Message as a platform where direct messaging, shared inboxes, automation, campaigns, compliance, and integrations come together.

The core mental model is:

1. Connect channels
   - WhatsApp Business, SMS, webchat, Facebook Messenger, Instagram Direct, Apple Messages for Business, custom channels, and other messaging channels are connected.

2. Centralise conversations
   - Incoming customer messages arrive in one shared inbox.

3. Organise
   - Conversations are labelled, assigned, routed, given statuses, and handled by teams.

4. Respond
   - Team members reply through the same environment while using conversation history and contact context.

5. Automate
   - Chatbots, knowledge bases, guided flows, trigger-based responses, and routing handle repetitive questions or first-line interactions.

6. Handover
   - Automation passes conversations to human team members when needed.

7. Proactively message
   - Teams send approved WhatsApp templates, SMS messages, notifications, reminders, alerts, or campaigns based on consent and channel rules.

8. Integrate
   - CRM, marketing, sales, service, and Spotler ecosystem data can trigger messages or record conversations at contact level.

9. Measure
   - Teams track delivery, reads, response rates, conversation volume, workload, and team performance.

Do not treat Spotler Message as only a chatbot, only a WhatsApp inbox, or only a campaign broadcasting tool. It combines one-to-one messaging, automation, proactive campaigns, compliance, collaboration, and integrations.

---

## Main Product Areas and Capabilities

### WhatsApp and Messaging Channels

Spotler Message is WhatsApp-first, but supports multiple messaging channels.

Supported channels include:

- WhatsApp Business
- SMS
- Webchat
- Facebook Messenger
- Instagram Direct
- Apple Messages for Business
- Custom channels

Design implication:

- WhatsApp is central to the product identity.
- Different channels may have different rules, formats, permissions, and expectations.
- Designs should preserve channel context rather than flattening all conversations into anonymous messages.

---

### Shared Inbox and Collaboration

Spotler Message gives teams one shared inbox for managing customer conversations.

Inbox and collaboration capabilities include:

- Shared inbox for all channels
- Assignment to teams
- Assignment to team members
- Labels
- Notes
- Conversation status
- User roles
- Permissions
- Conversation history
- Handover between automation and humans

Design implication:

- The inbox is an operational workspace, not just a message list.
- Users need to know who owns a conversation, what status it has, what channel it came from, and whether action is needed.
- Internal notes must be clearly separated from customer-facing replies.

---

### Conversation Management

Spotler Message supports one-to-one customer conversations.

Conversation management includes:

- Receiving messages
- Replying to customers
- Viewing complete conversation history
- Applying labels
- Adding notes
- Assigning conversations
- Updating status
- Routing to teams
- Escalating to humans from automation

Design implication:

- Conversation continuity matters.
- Contact-level history is important.
- The experience should feel personal and fast, not like anonymous ticket processing.

---

### Automation and Chatbots

Spotler Message supports automated conversation flows and chatbots.

Automation capabilities include:

- Automated conversation flows
- AI-powered chatbots
- Knowledge bases
- FAQ automation
- Trigger-based responses
- Trigger-based routing
- Automated handling of repetitive questions
- Seamless handover to team members

Design implication:

- Automation should reduce workload without removing human control.
- Handover to a team member should be clear and smooth.
- Chatbots are optional; users can start with human-only conversations and add automation later.
- Avoid making chatbot setup feel mandatory.

---

### Campaign Messaging

Spotler Message supports proactive campaigns and notifications through messaging channels.

Campaign capabilities include:

- Approved WhatsApp templates
- Notifications
- Marketing messages
- Broadcast messages
- Rich media
- Images
- Files
- Buttons
- Campaigns separate from live conversations
- Consent-based messaging

Design implication:

- Campaign messaging is different from active one-to-one conversations.
- Approved WhatsApp templates and permission-based messaging are important.
- Users need to understand when a message is a campaign, notification, or service conversation.

---

### Compliance and Security

Spotler Message supports secure and compliant messaging.

Compliance and security capabilities include:

- GDPR-friendly hosting in Europe
- WhatsApp Business API approval
- Permission-based messaging
- Approved WhatsApp templates
- Complete audit trail of conversations
- User roles
- Permissions

Design implication:

- Compliance is part of the product logic, especially for WhatsApp.
- Designs should not allow unrestricted promotional messaging without consent or template rules.
- Audit trail and permission controls matter for trust.

---

### Reporting and Insights

Spotler Message provides insight into service, campaign, and conversation performance.

Reporting may include:

- Delivery rates
- Read rates
- Response rates
- First response time
- Conversation volume
- Workload
- Team performance
- Campaign engagement
- Automation performance

Design implication:

- Reports should support operational improvement and campaign optimisation.
- Team leads need workload and response insights.
- Marketers need delivery, read, and campaign response insights.

---

### Integrations and Ecosystem

Spotler Message integrates with CRM systems, APIs, connector platforms, and other Spotler products.

Integration capabilities include:

- CRM contact synchronisation
- Recording conversations at contact level
- API and webhooks
- Zapier and Make integrations
- Triggering messages from customer behaviour or campaign actions
- Connecting messaging to email, webcare, CRM, and CDP workflows

Design implication:

- Integrations often determine what data is available and what actions can be triggered.
- Designs should clarify whether data is being imported, recorded, triggered, or synchronised.

---

## Important Product Objects and Concepts

### Contact

A person or customer who can have one-to-one messaging conversations and receive approved messages or campaigns.

### Conversation

A sequence of messages between an organisation and a contact through WhatsApp or another messaging channel.

### Message

An individual incoming or outgoing communication within a conversation or campaign.

### Channel

The medium used for communication, such as WhatsApp, SMS, webchat, Messenger, Instagram Direct, Apple Messages for Business, or a custom channel.

### Shared inbox

The central workspace where conversations from all connected channels are handled.

### Assignment

The process of assigning a conversation to a team or team member.

### Team

A group responsible for handling certain conversations, topics, channels, or workflows.

### Label

A marker used to categorise conversations by topic, issue, campaign, urgency, or type.

### Note

An internal comment added to a conversation for team collaboration. It should not be visible to the customer.

### Conversation status

The state of a conversation, such as new, open, assigned, waiting, resolved, or closed.

### Routing

The process of directing conversations to the correct team, team member, or automation flow.

### Handover

The transition from chatbot or automation to a human team member.

### Chatbot

An automated conversation agent that answers questions or guides users through flows.

### Knowledge base

A structured source of information used by AI-powered chatbots to answer questions.

### Automated conversation flow

A predefined guided conversation that can answer questions, collect input, route a contact, or trigger an action.

### Trigger

An event or condition that starts a response, flow, route, notification, or campaign.

### WhatsApp Business

The official business channel for communicating with customers via WhatsApp.

### WhatsApp Business API approval

The approval process needed to use WhatsApp Business API officially.

### Approved WhatsApp template

A pre-approved message template used for notifications or marketing messages within WhatsApp rules.

### Broadcast message

A proactive message sent to multiple recipients, separate from individual live conversations.

### Campaign

A planned proactive messaging activity, often using WhatsApp templates, SMS, or other channels.

### Rich media

Message content such as images, files, or buttons.

### Permission-based messaging

Messaging based on recipient consent and applicable channel rules.

### Audit trail

A complete history of conversations and actions for compliance, traceability, and accountability.

