## Feature Design Guidance

### Design for WhatsApp-first conversational messaging

Spotler Message is WhatsApp-first and should feel optimised for direct, fast, personal conversations.

Good designs should support:

- WhatsApp channel context
- Conversation history
- Quick replies
- Approved templates
- Rich media
- Human handover
- Consent-aware messaging

Avoid:

- Treating WhatsApp as generic email
- Hiding channel-specific rules
- Designing conversations as static records only

---

### Separate service conversations from campaign messaging

Spotler Message supports both one-to-one service and proactive campaigns.

Good designs should distinguish:

- Live conversation
- Broadcast message
- Notification
- Campaign
- Approved template
- Customer reply
- Routed follow-up

Avoid mixing campaign setup and active conversation handling in a way that confuses users.

---

### Make compliance visible but practical

Compliance matters, especially for WhatsApp and proactive messaging.

Good designs should show:

- Consent status
- Template approval status
- Permission-based messaging
- WhatsApp Business API approval
- Audit trail
- User permissions

Avoid:

- Allowing unrestricted marketing messages without consent
- Making compliance feel like hidden technical configuration
- Blocking users without explaining why

---

### Make automation and human handover work together

Automation should reduce workload while preserving a personal experience.

Good designs should show:

- Whether a conversation is handled by bot or human
- What the bot already asked or answered
- Why handover happened
- Who owns the conversation after handover
- What context the human receives

Avoid:

- Dead-end chatbot flows
- Handover without history
- Automation that hides customer intent

---

### Make routing and ownership clear

Routing determines who should respond.

Good designs should show:

- Assigned team
- Assigned team member
- Routing reason
- Conversation status
- Labels
- Priority where relevant

Avoid conversations sitting in unclear ownership states.

---

### Make reporting operational and campaign-relevant

Reporting should support both service and marketing.

Service reporting should help with:

- First response time
- Workload
- Conversation volume
- Team performance
- Automation impact

Campaign reporting should help with:

- Delivery rates
- Read rates
- Response rates
- Engagement
- Follow-up volume

Avoid one-size-fits-all reports that ignore the difference between service and campaign messaging.

---

## Important Assumptions to Preserve

Preserve these assumptions when generating product concepts, UX flows, screens, or feature ideas:

- Spotler Message is a WhatsApp-first messaging platform.
- It supports both one-to-one conversations and proactive messaging campaigns.
- It supports WhatsApp Business officially and compliantly.
- Shared inboxes are central.
- Conversations can be handled by humans, automation, or both.
- Chatbots are optional, not mandatory.
- Human handover must preserve conversation context.
- Campaign messaging uses approved WhatsApp templates and consent-based rules.
- Broadcast messages are separate from live conversations.
- Conversation history per contact matters.
- Routing, assignment, labels, notes, and status support team collaboration.
- Reporting should cover service, workload, delivery, reads, response, and campaign performance.
- GDPR-friendly hosting in Europe and audit trails are important.
- Integrations with CRM systems, APIs, webhooks, Zapier, Make, and Spotler products are important.
- Spotler Message is not the same as Spotler Engage; Engage focuses more broadly on webcare, publishing, and monitoring.

---

## Things Figma Make Should Avoid

Avoid these mistakes when designing Spotler Message features:

- Do not treat Spotler Message as only a chatbot tool.
- Do not treat Spotler Message as only a WhatsApp inbox.
- Do not treat Spotler Message as only a campaign broadcaster.
- Do not ignore WhatsApp Business compliance and template approval.
- Do not ignore consent and permission-based messaging.
- Do not mix broadcast campaigns and live conversations without clear distinction.
- Do not hide conversation ownership, assignment, status, or routing.
- Do not design automation without human handover.
- Do not make chatbots mandatory for all workflows.
- Do not lose context when automation hands over to a human.
- Do not treat all channels as identical.
- Do not call everything a “ticket” unless the feature explicitly needs ticket-like handling.
- Do not invent unrelated SaaS objects such as projects, boards, or generic tasks unless specifically needed.
- Do not replace product terminology with vague terms like “items” or “records”.
- Do not overfocus on visual styling; that belongs in the Design System Skill.

---

## Edge Cases and Product Logic to Consider

### Customer replies to a broadcast campaign

A campaign message may trigger a one-to-one conversation. Designs should route replies into the shared inbox with campaign context.

### Contact lacks consent

A contact may exist but cannot receive proactive campaign messages. Designs should show why sending is blocked or limited.

### WhatsApp template not approved

A proactive WhatsApp message may require an approved template. Designs should show approval state and alternatives.

### Automation cannot answer

A chatbot may fail to answer or detect intent. Designs should support smooth human handover.

### Human handover without context

Agents need to see what the bot already asked, answered, and collected. Avoid losing automation history.

### Multiple agents viewing the same conversation

Ownership and assignment should prevent duplicate responses.

### Conversation spans channels

A customer may interact via WhatsApp and later through webchat or another channel. Designs should preserve contact-level history where possible.

### High conversation volume

Large service volumes require routing, labels, assignment, automation, and workload insight.

### Channel-specific formatting

WhatsApp, SMS, webchat, and social messaging support different content types. Designs should not assume every channel supports images, files, buttons, or long messages equally.

### Campaign replies create service workload

Marketing campaigns can increase incoming service conversations. Designs should connect campaign planning with inbox capacity where relevant.

### Audit trail needed

Compliance or operations teams may need traceability of who sent, replied, routed, or changed a conversation.

---

## Relationship to the Separate Design System Skill

This Skill provides product context only.

Use this Skill for:

- Product behaviour
- Workflows
- Messaging terminology
- User roles
- Conversation logic
- Shared inbox logic
- WhatsApp Business logic
- Campaign messaging logic
- Automation and chatbot logic
- Human handover logic
- Compliance and permission logic
- Reporting and operational insight
- Integration context
- Feature-specific product assumptions

Do not use this Skill for:

- Component styling
- Typography
- Colour usage
- Layout systems
- Spacing
- Icons
- Button styles
- Form styling
- Navigation visual patterns
- Design tokens

For those topics, use the separate Spotler Design System Skill or attached Design System documentation.

When both Skills are available:

- Use this Skill to decide what the feature should do and how it should behave.
- Use the Design System Skill to decide how the feature should look and be constructed visually.

---

## Final Checklist for Figma Make

Before generating or redesigning a Spotler Message feature, check:

- Does the design use Spotler Message terminology?
- Is the relevant user role clear?
- Is the feature grounded in WhatsApp, messaging channels, shared inboxes, conversations, automation, campaigns, routing, compliance, reporting, or integrations?
- Does the design distinguish one-to-one conversations from broadcast campaigns?
- Does it make channel context visible?
- Does it make assignment, ownership, labels, notes, and status clear where relevant?
- Does it support automation and human handover without losing context?
- Does it account for WhatsApp template approval and consent?
- Does it treat chatbots as optional rather than mandatory?
- Does it support CRM, API, webhook, connector, or Spotler ecosystem integrations where relevant?
- Does reporting support both service performance and campaign performance?
- Does it avoid generic SaaS assumptions?
- Does it avoid visual styling decisions that belong to the Design System Skill?

---

